{"acceptedResponses":["C"],"answer":"C","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(5\\sqrt5\\)","B":"\\(\\sqrt5\\)","C":"\\(25\\sqrt5\\)","D":"\\(125\\)","E":"\\(25\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1958_AMC_12_Q20","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h039"],"contentHash":"b622715dbe2ec5734c2f463c6b90c135758613c918ca24b635d20a48897fb4cc","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-base-two","label":"Compare powers of two and evaluate the final fractional power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-two":["cnm_h042","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h042","cnm_h039"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-exponent-with-logs","label":"Find the exponent using logs and evaluate the final fractional power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-exponent-with-logs":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_h039"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_015","cnm_042","cnm_167"],"id":"factor-and-compare-powers","label":"Factor the common power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-compare-powers":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039","cnm_015","cnm_042","cnm_167"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_015","cnm_042"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":20,"question":"If \\(4^x-4^{x-1}=24,\\) then \\((2x)^x\\) equals:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"The exponents differ by one, so \\(4^x=4\\cdot4^{x-1}\\). This lets us factor the subtraction:\n\\[\n4^x-4^{x-1}\n=(4-1)4^{x-1}=24.\n\\]\nThus \\(4^{x-1}=8\\). Writing both sides as powers of \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n2^{2x-2}=2^3.\n\\]\nPowers of \\(2\\) increase strictly with their exponents, so their equality implies\n\\[\n2x-2=3,\n\\qquad x=\\frac52.\n\\]\nNow \\(2x=5>0\\), and the fractional exponent gives\n\\[\n(2x)^x=5^{5/2}=5^2\\sqrt{5}=25\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice C.","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039"],"explanation":"The exponents differ by one, so \\(4^x=4\\cdot4^{x-1}\\). This lets us factor the subtraction:\n\\[\n4^x-4^{x-1}\n=(4-1)4^{x-1}=24.\n\\]\nThus \\(4^{x-1}=8\\). Writing both sides as powers of \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n2^{2x-2}=2^3.\n\\]\nPowers of \\(2\\) increase strictly with their exponents, so their equality implies\n\\[\n2x-2=3,\n\\qquad x=\\frac52.\n\\]\nNow \\(2x=5>0\\), and the fractional exponent gives\n\\[\n(2x)^x=5^{5/2}=5^2\\sqrt{5}=25\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice C.","id":"factor-and-compare-powers","label":"Factor the common power","legacyRouteId":"factor-and-compare-powers","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1958_AMC_12_Q20.json","sha256":"15cc03eb5b33ed208bee288ec6488b16b0d98fb5e7200b168f97ae3d195d0544"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1958_AMC_12_Q20#factor-and-compare-powers","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_h042","cnm_h039"],"id":"common-base-two","label":"Compare powers of two and evaluate the final fractional power","legacyRouteId":"common-base-two","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1958_AMC_12_Q20.json","sha256":"15cc03eb5b33ed208bee288ec6488b16b0d98fb5e7200b168f97ae3d195d0544"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","source":"Existing reviewed concept route","sourceRef":"amc/1